<article class="prose">
  <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
  <h1>Targets</h1>
  <p class="lede">A target tells a category how much it needs. There are three kinds.</p>

  <section class="guide-split">
    <div class="copy">
  <h2>Monthly</h2>
  <p>Assign a fixed amount every month. A bill of $1,500 still asks for $1,500 next month, even if some of this month is left over. Leftover available stays in the category. It does not reduce the next target.</p>

  <h2>Fill up to</h2>
  <p>Keep the category topped up to an amount. If groceries are set to $600 and $100 is already available, this month only needs $500 more.</p>

  <h2>Save by date</h2>
  <p>Have a total by a month and year. ZxBudget splits what is still needed across the months that remain. The date is a month, not a day.</p>

    </div>
    <div class="shots">
      <figure class="step-shot">
        <img src="/images/guides/home.jpg" width="800" height="1740" alt="Home in the sample budget, with Monthly targets showing 24 of 25 at target.">
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>Snooze</h2>
  <p>Snooze silences the underfunded reminder for a month without deleting the target. Auto-Assign skips a snoozed category. The target is still there when the snooze ends.</p>

  <h2>Auto-Assign</h2>
  <p>On the Budget tab, <strong>Auto-Assign</strong> can fill this month’s categories from the targets you set. The main action is one tap, and it opens a preview before anything changes. You still choose whether to apply it. Other actions sit beside that one.</p>
</article>
